  "diagnosis": "The failing sweeps are shape mismatches (46 vs 23, 131072 vs 65536), not value mismatches: the case module's `_doc_float_neg` flips the sign bit of the operand array but the expected array is twice the length of the input, so the comparison is between arrays of different shapes. The document's float rule (E_NEG_FLOAT_FUNC_0010: same magnitude, opposite sign, NaN stays NaN) is not what the case module computes; the case set is at fault, not the document or the implementation.",
  "instruction": "Fix `_doc_float_neg` in the case module. It currently returns an array of twice the input's length (the sign-flip view is being concatenated or broadcast against the input rather than replacing it). The expected value must be exactly the operand with its sign bit flipped, element for element, same shape and dtype: `b = np.ascontiguousarray(a).view(uint) ^ sign; return b.view(dt)` must produce an array of the same shape as `a`. Check that `sign` is a scalar of the right width and that no `np.concatenate`/broadcast is applied to the result. After the fix, re-run `sweep_float_specials` and `sweep_float16_exhaustive`; the expected shapes must be (23,) and (65536,) respectively, matching the input. NaN comparison stays as-is (NaN equals NaN). No change to the document or the implementation.",
